# Nurture Flutter App

Flutter client for the Nurture health/food-tracking backend
(`~/all/projects/nurture_backend`, Django + DRF + JWT, prod at http://72.61.91.102).

**Always read `docs/PROJECT.md` first** — stage roadmap, current status, decisions.
Update it at the end of every stage.

## Architecture (feature-first clean architecture)

- `lib/features/{feature}/{presentation,domain,data}`
  - presentation: screens, widgets, Riverpod controllers (`AsyncNotifier`)
  - domain: entities (immutable, manual `copyWith`), repository interfaces
  - data: repository implementations, API calls via `core/api/api_client.dart`
- `lib/core/`: config (API base URL via `--dart-define=API_BASE_URL`), theme,
  ApiClient (JWT header + auto-refresh on 401), shared Riverpod providers.
- Widgets max 100 lines; extract sub-widgets into `widgets/` folders.
- No logic in widgets/setState — all logic in controllers/repositories.
- `const` constructors everywhere possible. Descriptive variable names.

## Workflow (stage-gated, mirrors backend)

Stages: F0 auth/scaffold → F1 profile → F2 foods+photo → F3 diary/weight
→ F4 progress charts. Build one stage, run `flutter analyze` + `flutter test`,
commit, STOP for user confirmation before the next stage.

## Backend API notes

- All responses flat JSON. Auth: `POST /api/auth/{register,login,refresh}/`,
  `GET|PATCH /api/auth/me/`. Errors: `{"detail": ...}` or `{field: [msgs]}`.
- Foods global (`/api/foods/`, paginated; `search/?q=` returns plain list).
- Logs/weight/progress are user-scoped by the JWT automatically.
- Android uses cleartext HTTP (no TLS on VPS yet).
